---
description: Building a RustStream service without the `macros` feature (hand-written handlers, registration and main)
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- Everything the attribute macros produce can be written by hand. `macros` is a convenience
  feature, not a layer: `#[subscriber]` expands into calls on the same public runtime API, so a
  service without it loses no capability that does not come *from* a declaration.
- What the attribute emits for `async fn handle(order: &Order) -> HandlerResult` is, in order: a
  unit struct named after the function, an `impl Handler` whose `async fn handle` carries the body,
  and the `Declared` + `SubscriberDef` impls that let `include` mount it. Every one of those traits
  is public, so **how far you write it out is a choice, not a limit**.
- So a handler is a named type implementing `Handler<M, C, S>`, whose `async fn handle(&self, msg:
  &M, ctx: &mut Context<'_, C, S>) -> Settle` is an ordinary async method. What a closure would
  capture becomes a field on that type.
- **Level one** - stop at the body trait and mount with `b.include(subscriber(source, handler))`.
  There is one constructor per attribute form, and every form has one: `subscriber`, `raw`,
  `batch`, `raw_batch`, `batch_with_headers`, `replying(..).to(..)`, `raw_replying(..).to(..)`,
  `batch_replying(..).to(..)`, `with_slots`, `with_seek`, `batch_with_slots`, `batch_with_seek`,
  `replying_with_slots(..).to(..)`, `raw_replying_with_slots(..).to(..)` and
  `batch_replying_with_slots(..).to(..)`. A body implemented for one concrete application state
  takes the `_in` variant (`subscriber_in`, `raw_in`, `batch_in`, `raw_batch_in`,
  `batch_with_headers_in`, `replying_in`, `raw_replying_in`, `batch_replying_in`); the slot and
  seek forms need none, their bodies being checked at the mount. Nothing else to implement; the
  registration metadata is derived from the source and the handler's input type.
- **Level two** - implement `SubscriberDef` plus `Declared`, and `b.include(def)` works on your own
  type with the whole mount surface: the settings builder (`.name`, `.workers`, `.on_failure`,
  `.buffered`), `.publisher(..)`, `.out(marker, ..)` and `.mount()`. The settings live on
  `Declared::Settings`, which must be a `SubscriberBuilder` - `SubscriberBuilder::new(def, source)`
  is public for exactly this. Implementing `IncludeDef` alone routes through the blanket
  `impl<T: IncludeDef> Declared for T`, whose `Settings = Self`: that serves the raw forms, but a
  decoded one resolves its codec through the builder, so a bare definition does not mount there -
  and the steps (`NameStep`, `WorkersStep`, `FailureStep`, `BufferedStep`) are implemented for
  `SubscriberBuilder`, not for a bare definition, so that route has no settings surface either.
- The derives are equally reproducible, and reading their expansion is the fastest way to get one
  right: `#[derive(Outgoing)]` writes `OutgoingDestination` plus a `MessageHeaders` contract
  (`NoHeaders` or `WithHeaders<H>`), `#[derive(OutSlot)]` writes `OutSlot` plus the
  `ContainsMessage` memberships, `#[derive(OutMessages)]` writes `OutMessages` over an enum's
  variants, and `#[derive(FromRef)]` writes one `FromRef` impl per field.
- The one thing genuinely lost with the feature off is the attribute surface itself: nothing reads
  a clause for you, so what the attribute would have declared you write as a trait impl.
- `use ruststream::prelude::*;` still works: the glob's macro re-exports are themselves
  feature-gated, so the rest of it comes in unchanged - the builder, `Context`, `HandlerResult`,
  `Name`, `Publisher`, `PublishExt`, `TypedPublisher`, and the whole manual path (every form
  constructor listed above with its `_in` variants, the body traits `Handler` / `SliceHandler` /
  `RawSliceHandler` / `SliceHandlerWithHeaders` / `Reply` / `BatchReply` / `SlotsHandler` /
  `SlotsSliceHandler` / `SlotsReply` / `SlotsBatchReply`, plus `Settle`, `BatchResult`,
  `FailurePolicies` / `FailurePolicy`, `Workers`, `Unnamed`, `Out`, `Seek`, `DefaultSlot`,
  `RouterDef` and `nonzero!`).
- Mount with `b.include(subscriber(source, handler))` (the portable form: the source resolves at
  startup), or `b.handle(subscriber, handler, metadata)` when you already hold an open subscriber.
  Routers have the same `include` method, so grouping still works.
- Write `main` yourself: `#[tokio::main]` plus `app.run().await`. The attribute's CLI (`run`,
  `asyncapi gen`) is what you give up; `run_until(..)` and `start()` are plain methods.
- State is reached through `ctx.state()`, headers through `ctx.headers()`, and publishing through a
  publisher the handler holds or reads from the state - the same objects the extractors would have
  handed over.

## A whole service, macro-free

```rust
use std::error::Error;

use ruststream::memory::MemoryBroker;
use ruststream::prelude::*;
use serde::Deserialize;

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
struct Order {
    id: u64,
}

/// The handler body. The attribute would have generated exactly this struct, named after
/// the function it decorates.
struct Handle;

impl Handler<Order> for Handle {
    async fn handle(&self, order: &Order, _ctx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Settle {
        tracing::info!(order.id, "processing order");
        HandlerResult::ack().into()
    }
}

fn app() -> RustStream {
    RustStream::new(AppInfo::new("orders", "0.1.0")).with_broker(MemoryBroker::new(), |b| {
        b.include(subscriber("orders", Handle));
    })
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    app().run().await?;
    Ok(())
}
```

The method returns `Settle`, not `HandlerResult`: the attribute inserts the `IntoSettle` conversion
around the body, so a hand-written impl does it itself - `.into()` on any outcome, or
`HandlerResult::ack().and_after(..)` when there is post-settle work.

A body with nothing to await returns the future directly (`fn handle(..) -> impl Future<Output =
Settle> + Send` with `ready(..)`), because `clippy::unused_async_trait_impl` is denied and a
handler that awaits nothing is not async. **Keep anything that can panic inside the future**: the
dispatcher calls `handler.handle(..)` and only then wraps the result in `catch_unwind`, so a panic
raised in the synchronous part escapes the guard and kills the task instead of settling by
`on_failure(panic = ..)`. Where the body can panic, write `fn handle(..) -> impl Future { async
move { .. } }` rather than computing before `ready(..)`.

`run()` runs until `SIGINT` / `SIGTERM`; `run_until(future)` drives shutdown from a future you own
(a test signal, a timeout), and `start()` hands back a `RunningApp` for hosting the service beside
another foreground server.

## Decoding

A constructor resolves the decode codec off the mount surface, the same ladder the attribute path
uses: the scope's (`with_broker_codec`), the router chain's (`Router::with_codec`), else
`DefaultCodec`. `.codec(..)` overrides it for one registration, and `.on_failure(..)` carries the
decode-failure policy. (`typed(codec, handler)` is the wrapper underneath - it decodes the delivery
and calls the inner handler with `&T` - and the one you build yourself for the `handle` SPI below,
which reads no scope codec.)

```rust
use ruststream::codec::CborCodec;

// the decode-failure policy, the manual counterpart of `on_failure(decode = ..)`
b.include(
    subscriber("orders", Handle)
        .on_failure(FailurePolicies::default().with_decode(FailurePolicy::Retry)),
);

// this registration's own decode codec, overriding the scope's
b.include(subscriber("audit", Handle).codec(CborCodec));
```

## Raw bytes

The `raw` constructor takes a `Handler<[u8]>`, so the payload arrives undecoded and no codec is
involved. This is the shape `#[subscriber(.., raw)]` produces, and the only subscriber form
available with no codec feature at all (`raw_batch` is its batch counterpart, over
`RawSliceHandler`).

```rust
struct Ingest;

impl Handler<[u8]> for Ingest {
    async fn handle(&self, payload: &[u8], _ctx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Settle {
        parse(payload);
        HandlerResult::ack().into()
    }
}

b.include(raw("frames", Ingest));
```

Use `b.handle(subscriber, handler, meta)` instead when the broker hands you an open subscriber
before registration (how you obtain one is broker-specific; the in-memory broker's `subscribe` is
a plain synchronous call):

```rust
use ruststream::memory::MemoryMessage;
use ruststream::runtime::HandlerMetadata;

// The SPI takes a handler over the broker's own message, not over `[u8]`.
struct Frames;

impl Handler<MemoryMessage> for Frames {
    async fn handle(&self, msg: &MemoryMessage, _ctx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Settle {
        parse(msg.payload());
        HandlerResult::ack().into()
    }
}

let subscriber = b.broker().subscribe("orders");
b.handle(subscriber, Frames, HandlerMetadata::raw("orders"));
```

## Dependencies and state

A closure captures; a named handler holds. Anything the body needs that is fixed at registration
becomes a field, and everything the runtime owns per delivery comes off the context - there is no
`State<T>` parameter and no `FromRef` derive to make one resolve.

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};

use ruststream::codec::Codec; // the trait, for `encode`

/// Held: fixed at registration. Read off the context: owned by the runtime.
struct Confirm {
    seen: Arc<AtomicU64>,
}

struct AppState {
    receipts: MemoryPublisher,
}

impl Handler<Order, (), AppState> for Confirm {
    async fn handle(&self, order: &Order, ctx: &mut Context<'_, (), AppState>) -> Settle {
        self.seen.fetch_add(order.id, Ordering::SeqCst);

        let payload = JsonCodec
            .encode(&Receipt { order_id: order.id })
            .expect("encode");
        if ctx
            .state()
            .receipts
            .raw(&payload)
            .to("receipts")
            .publish()
            .await
            .is_err()
        {
            return HandlerResult::retry().into();
        }
        HandlerResult::ack().into()
    }
}

// `subscriber(source, body)` binds a body over the unit application state, so a body pinned to
// `AppState` takes the `_in` variant: it reads the state off the `Handler` impl, and the mount
// checks it against the app's.
let app = RustStream::new(AppInfo::new("orders", "0.1.0"))
    .on_startup(async move |()| Ok::<_, std::convert::Infallible>(AppState { receipts }))
    .with_broker(MemoryBroker::new(), |b| {
        b.include(subscriber_in("orders", Confirm { seen }));
    });
```

`Context<'_, C, S>` names what the attribute would have inferred: `C` the broker's per-delivery
context, `S` the application state. Both default to `()`, so a handler needing neither writes
`Context<'_>`.

## Headers

`Headers<T>` is an extractor, so the manual path parses the map itself. It is the same call the
extractor makes, and it returns a `Result` you settle on yourself:

```rust
let Ok(meta) = ctx.headers().to_typed::<ChunkMeta>() else {
    return HandlerResult::drop().into();
};
```

`ctx.headers_mut()` is the working copy middleware enriches, and `HeaderMap::insert_typed(&value)`
writes a contract back into it.

## Publishing

`message(&value)` needs `T: OutgoingDestination`, which is what `#[derive(Outgoing)]` implements -
so without the derive the typed publish builder has nothing to resolve a destination from. Two
routes remain, and both are the ones the builder itself ends in:

```rust
use ruststream::codec::Codec; // `encode` is a trait method

// encode with the codec, then send the bytes to a destination you name
let bytes = JsonCodec.encode(&receipt)?;
publisher.raw(&bytes).to("receipts").publish().await?;

// or build the message and call the Publisher trait method directly
publisher
    .publish(OutgoingMessage::new("receipts", bytes.as_ref()).with_headers(headers))
    .await?;
```

`TypedPublisher`, `after_startup`, publish transforms and `publish_layer` are all runtime API and
work unchanged. The reply clause becomes `replying(source, body).to(dest)` over an `impl Reply<T>`
with `type Out`, and the `Out` slot parameter becomes
`with_slots::<T, (Marker,), _, _>(source, body)` over an `impl SlotsHandler<T, Slots>` (both at
once: `replying_with_slots(..).to(..)` over `SlotsReply`); the include site still binds each slot
with `.out(marker, policy)` or `.publisher(policy)` and commits with `.mount()`.

## Middleware

A layer is the same named-type shape one level up: `Layer<H>` wraps an inner handler, and the
wrapper implements `Handler` by delegating. This is the form the bundled layers already use, so
nothing here is macro-dependent.

```rust
struct Counted<H> {
    inner: H,
    count: Arc<AtomicU64>,
}

impl<M, C, S, H> Handler<M, C, S> for Counted<H>
where
    M: Sync,
    C: Send,
    S: Send + Sync,
    H: Handler<M, C, S>,
{
    async fn handle(&self, msg: &M, ctx: &mut Context<'_, C, S>) -> Settle {
        self.count.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);
        self.inner.handle(msg, ctx).await
    }
}
```

## Reaching the rest: write the definition out

`b.handle` registers with the default failure policies and no settings, because it takes a handler,
not a definition. It also never reads the scope codec, so `with_broker_codec` and
`Router::with_codec` are inert on a `handle` registration - the codec is the one you named at
`typed(..)`.

A `Router` reaches a little further without a definition: `Router::workers(Workers::pool(n))` /
`Workers::keyed(n)` is the functional counterpart of the `workers(..)` clause (`BrokerScope` has no
worker control at all). Beyond that, `batch(source, body)` and `raw_batch(source, body)` are the
macro-free paths for the batch shapes, and both resolve the chain's codec.

Every form token has a constructor, so no form is reachable only through the definition traits: a
body pinned to a concrete application state takes an `_in` constructor, a typed broker context is
read off the body's own impl (and named with `.context::<K>()` on the `with_seek` /
`replying_with_slots` chains), and the injection, batch-publishing, raw-reply and headers-contract
shapes each have their own constructor. The schema opt-ins ride the same chain: `.describe(..)`,
`.documented()`, `.documented_headers::<H>()` and `.message()`. What is left for the definition
traits is being the attribute's own expansion target, and a fully custom definition - one whose
source, metadata or settings are computed rather than named - so implement one and the whole mount
surface opens up on your own type.

```rust
use ruststream::runtime::{
    Declared, FailurePolicies, FailurePolicy, Fixed, Open, SubscriberBuilder, SubscriberDef,
    SubscriberSettings, forms,
};

// This exact case is shorter as `subscriber("orders", Handle).on_failure(..)`; the shape below is
// what a definition carrying its own declaration looks like.
impl SubscriberDef for Handle {
    type Input = /* the decode kind */;
    type Context = ();
    type Handler = Self;
    type Source = Name;

    fn source(&self) -> Self::Source { Name::new("orders") }
    fn into_handler(self) -> Self { self }
}

// `Declared` is where the settings live, and its `Settings` has to be a builder. This is
// verbatim what the expansion emits: `SubscriberBuilder::new(self, source)` plus the chain the
// attribute's clauses parsed into.
impl Declared for Handle {
    type Form = forms::Subscribing;
    type Settings = SubscriberBuilder<Self, Name, (Open, Fixed, Open)>;

    fn declare(self) -> Self::Settings {
        SubscriberBuilder::new(self, Name::new("orders"))
            .on_failure(FailurePolicies::default().with_decode(FailurePolicy::Retry))
    }
}

b.include(Handle);
```

The two routes are mutually exclusive: a direct `impl Declared` conflicts with the blanket
`impl<T: IncludeDef> Declared for T`, so pick one per definition. The builder route is the one that
scales - `SubscriberBuilder` forwards `SubscriberDef`, `InjectDef`/`InjectCall`,
`PublishingDef`/`PublishingCall`, `BatchPublishingDef`/`BatchPublishingCall` and
`HasSlots`/`BindSlots`, so the same shape carries every handler form, injected slots included.
It does mean the source is written twice, in `declare()` and in the def's own `source()`, which is
what the attribute writes too.

The settings state rides in the `Settings` type (`AllOpen` when the declaration fixed none,
`Fixed` in the slot for each one it did), which is what makes naming the same setting again at the
mount site a compile error rather than a silent override. Put a setting in `declare` to keep it
next to the handler, or leave the state open and let the mount site name it - the same choice the
attribute gives you between a clause and a builder call.

Read the expansion in `crates/ruststream-macros/src/expand.rs` before writing one of these: it
names the exact `Input` kind, `Context` and form marker per handler shape, and it is the only place
the whole mapping is written down. A definition that wants the attribute's own settings surface
instead of the blanket can implement `Declared` directly and return
`SubscriberBuilder::new(self, source)` with the chain applied, which is literally what the macro
emits.

The same holds for the derives: implement `OutgoingDestination` + `MessageHeaders` for a declared
message, `OutSlot` + `ContainsMessage` for a slot marker, `OutMessages` for a set, `FromRef` per
state field. The schema hooks on `SubscriberDef` are what feed the generated AsyncAPI document, so
a hand-written definition contributes the same schemas.

Enabling `macros` remains the shorter road, and the two styles compose in one application: a
macro-free registration and an `include(def)` sit in the same `with_broker` scope.
